    "26": {
      "verdict": "alternative",
      "other": "Some students no longer draw in the book until they are sure a drawing will work. / The author uses old sketchbooks to find where a present problem first appeared.",
      "reason": "Two choices both support the claim that a sketchbook is useful 'only so long as its owner is willing to let it be bad'. The students who won't draw until they are sure show the negative case: once pages must be good, the book stops being used to think. The author returning to old 'clumsy lines' to trace a current problem shows the positive case: the bad pages are exactly what makes the book useful. Whichever one is the key, the other is a reasonable answer a careful student could defend."
    },
